//? Title: NMS, anchor matching and box coding
//? Subtitle: Tensor computations, explicit checks and reproducible local figures
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Object detectors produce overlapping candidate boxes that must be assigned to targets, refined and reduced to a small detection set. This report checks tensor suppression, RPN/SSD matching, reversible box coding and stable proposal losses on hand-checkable fixtures, then exposes the optimizer and paired-checkpoint lifecycle.
)
\toc

Source example: `examples/cpp/source/nn/1_convolutional_neural_networks/1.6_object_detection/nms_matching.cpp`.

> The method

For boxes A and B, IoU is intersection area divided by union area, with zero for an empty union. NMS costs quadratic overlap work in the worst case; matching stores an anchor-by-target overlap matrix. Neither operation is differentiable selection. Regression coding and proposal losses retain tensor gradients; training uses explicit Adam steps below.

The editable concept diagram is `diagrams/nms_matching_concept.tikz`. Regeneration uses
LaTeX with PGF/TikZ and standalone, plus dvisvgm; exports use the saved SVG.

```{bash, results="output", file="figures/nms_matching_concept.svg"}
set -eu
mkdir -p build
latex -interaction=batchmode -halt-on-error -output-directory=build diagrams/nms_matching_concept.tikz > build/nms_matching_concept.compile.log 2>&1
dvisvgm --no-fonts --bbox=10 --output=build/nms_matching_concept.svg build/nms_matching_concept.dvi > build/nms_matching_concept.render.log 2>&1
printf 'Rendered local concept diagram.\n'
```
